A storyteller and a leader both inherit something people already feel they own. The filmmaker inherits characters, scenes, and the private Middle-earth each reader has been carrying around. The leader inherits institutions, promises, and competing ideas of what the country is supposed to be. Both have to decide what they owe the inheritance and who gets a say in what happens next.

Take a Lord of the Rings adaptation. Suppose test audiences want Aragorn to defeat Sauron in a duel. You could give them the duel. You could also ask what they wanted from it. Maybe they wanted Aragorn’s courage to matter at the climax. The march on the Black Gate already gives him that. He risks everything to draw Sauron’s attention while Frodo and Sam carry the burden nobody else can. The audience can be right about what they’re missing and leave the storyteller room to decide how to give it to them. That’s a real parallel with representative democracy.

And your political comparisons get more interesting when you let them overlap. They answer different questions about the same production.

Question	How it might play out
Who decides?	One director has final cut. A writers’ collective votes. Fans elect representatives to a council with defined powers.
What binds the decision-maker?	The source text can function like a constitution, with a lot left open to interpretation. If a sacred text and its authorized interpreters govern, the analogy is closer to theocracy.
Who owns the work and its proceeds?	Private investors can own a production with a democratically run writers’ room. A worker-owned studio can delegate final cut to one director. The economics and creative authority can vary independently.
What survives a revision?	A conservative adaptation might preserve the familiar scenes. Another might change scenes to preserve the story’s moral structure. Both are conserving something different.

That last one goes back to your point about forgetting the word and keeping the experience. An adaptation can reproduce a beloved line and lose the feeling that made it beloved. It can also write a new line that lands with the same weight. The nostalgia question is what are people trying to get back? Sometimes it’s a specific image. Sometimes it’s who they were when they first saw it. And sometimes it’s the experience of being surprised, which is exactly what a faithful replay can have the hardest time giving them.